9	// handler that reads `event.results[i][0].transcript` and delivers it has
10	// therefore just handed the caller a half-heard fragment — and will hand it the
11	// next fragment, and the next, several times per utterance.
12	//
13	// The failure is silent in the usual way: nothing throws, nothing logs,
14	// and the transcript even looks plausible. The app simply acts on words the user
15	// had not finished saying, and repeats itself as the guess is revised — the
16	// `"heart heart"` shape a mid-utterance pause monitor exists to prevent. Interim
17	// results are a "still speaking" SIGNAL; they are not input.
18	//
19	// The rule is deliberately narrow in three ways, each killing a false alarm a
20	// whole-file grep for `interimResults` makes:
21	//
22	//   1. The assigned value is read. `interimResults = false` (or 0/null/undefined)
23	//      turns the flag OFF, every result is final, and there is nothing to gate —
24	//      firing there is pure noise. Anything else counts as enabling, including a
25	//      computed flag (`rec.interimResults = pauseResetMs > 0`), because a check
26	//      cannot know it is false and the handler must survive it being true.
27	//   2. Only a file that SPELLS OUT its result handler is judged. A config module
28	//      that sets the flag and hands the recognizer on, and a file that delegates
29	//      (`rec.onresult = this.handleResult`), both keep their gate in some other
30	//      file; this rule has no honest opinion about a handler it cannot see, and
31	//      a check that guesses is worse than one that asks a simple, honest
32	//      question.
33	//   3. Comments are stripped first, so a comment naming the trap — the
34	//      paragraphs above included — cannot fire the rule.
35	//
36	// `isFinal` anywhere in the file counts as the gate: it is the ONLY thing in the
37	// Web Speech API that distinguishes an interim result from a final one, so there
38	// is no second spelling to look for, and reading it at all means the distinction
39	// was considered.
40	
41	// `x.interimResults = V` (a property assignment, not `==`/`===`) or the
42	// object-literal key `interimResults: V`, with V captured up to the end of the
43	// value — enough to tell a literal `false` from everything else.
44	const FLAG = /\binterimResults\s*(?:(?<![=!<>])=(?!=)|:)\s*([^,;\n)}]*)/g;
45	const OFF = /^(?:false|0|null|undefined)$/;
46	
47	// Both legal wirings, each split at the point where the handler ARGUMENT begins.
48	// This rule needs the argument itself, not merely the fact of a wiring, so
49	// `lib.mjs`'s `wires` — which answers only "is `result` wired here?" — is not
50	// enough; the two forms are still both accepted, as everywhere in this pack.
51	const WIRES_RESULT = [
52	  /\.\s*onresult\s*=\s*/g,
53	  /addEventListener\s*\(\s*['"`]result['"`]\s*,\s*/g,
54	];
55	
